  "query_preview": "..\nClear skies this morning with some upper level moisture moving\ninto the area bringing chances for high clouds to develop across\nthe area this afternoon. Building pressure over Texas will\nincrease high temperatures here in the Southwest this week\nbringing near to slightly above normal values. For this time of\nyear, normal high temperatures are around 98-99 degrees. \n\nA weak mid-level low will move into northern Baja into Sonora, \nMX starting Tuesday afternoon leading to south to southeasterly\nflow. Moisture will move into New Mexico tomorrow kicking off \nscattered thunderstorms with outflows spreading low-level \nmoisture into our eastern areas Tuesday night into Wednesday.\nPWAT values will increase to range between 0.8 to 1.0 inches\ncombined with ample instability will allow for cloud build ups\nand a chance (15-60%) for afternoon thunderstorms mainly over \nthe mountains of Greenlee, Graham and Cochise counties Wednesday\nand Thursday. Given the moisture profile, this will be a mix of\nboth wet and dry thunderstorms with the wetter storms focused \nover the White Mountains and high elevations of the Chiricahuas.\nDry thunderstorms will be focused to the western edge of the \nwestward moisture push (western Graham and Cochise counties).\n\nMoisture departs the area on Friday with lingering storm \nchances in the White Mountains, otherwise dry conditions under \nwesterly flow and broad troughiness over the western US. Temperatures\nby Friday and the weekend will be a few degrees cooler and \nslightly below normal as normal high temperatures in Tucson \nreach 100 degrees by next Saturday.",
